<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glyptothek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Munich Glyptothek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Glyptothek is a museum of ancient Greek and Roman sculpture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Ludwig I of Bavaria</a> and built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-von-klenze">Leo von Klenze</a> between 1816 and 1830. It was the first public museum in Munich. Its collection includes the pediment sculptures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-aphaia">Temple of Aphaia</a> on Aegina and the Barberini Faun.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glyptothek.jpg" alt="Glyptothek" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: High Contrast · CC BY 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glyptothek_in_M%C3%BCnchen_in_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
